#N canvas 20 20 2760 1680 12;
#X text 20 20 u_tempo -- the MASTER REFERENCE. It owns the master BPM \, the pulse oscillator that MIDI clock is cut from \, and the transport. It is NOT the clock: Cut It runs poly-tempo and every part that needs a beat owns a c_clock instance of its own. See ref-conventions.md \, Poly-tempo., f 58;
#X text 20 140 creation args: none. inlets: none. outlets: none. It talks on tempo \, clock \, start \, stop \, panic \, disp and err \, and out of two MIDI ports., f 58;
#X obj 20 300 r tempo;
#X obj 20 345 t f f;
#X obj 20 400 clip 5 600;
#X obj 20 445 t f f f;
#X obj 450 470 s \$0-clamped;
#X obj 250 505 s \$0-bpm;
#X obj 20 545 / 60;
#X obj 20 590 * 24;
#X obj 20 645 phasor~;
#X obj 20 705 threshold~ 0.5 0 0.1 0;
#X obj 20 770 t b b;
#X obj 20 830 f;
#X obj 20 875 + 1;
#X obj 20 920 mod 24;
#X obj 140 965 t f f;
#X obj 140 1030 select 0;
#X obj 140 1075 s clock;
#X obj 200 555 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 200 600 0;
#X obj 330 615 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 330 660 0;
#X obj 480 715 r \$0-reset;
#X msg 480 760 23;
#X msg 300 830 248;
#X obj 300 875 s \$0-rt;
#N canvas 40 40 1200 840 range-check 0;
#X text 20 20 The raw value arrives AFTER the clamped one -- it comes off the left outlet of a trigger whose right outlet ran the whole clamp chain first. So the comparison always has something true to compare against \, with no delay object anywhere., f 58;
#X obj 20 170 inlet;
#X obj 20 270 !=;
#X obj 240 170 r \$0-clamped;
#X obj 20 220 change;
#X obj 20 320 select 1;
#X msg 20 370 warn u_tempo bpm-out-of-range;
#X obj 20 420 s err;
#X text 20 490 THE change IS ON THE VALUE \, NOT ON THE VERDICT \, and getting that backwards was a real bug. Filtering the out-of-range FLAG means 500 warns and a 5 sent straight after it does not -- the flag never changed \, so a second and quite different fault is silent. Filtering the VALUE keeps the only case worth suppressing: the same number arriving over and over from a control parked past the end., f 58;
#X text 20 660 select 1's reject carries the 0 it did not match and goes nowhere \, which is the safe way to treat any reject. u_err's alert text is one symbol of 21 characters or fewer \; bpm-out-of-range is 16., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 4 0;
#X connect 4 0 2 0;
#X connect 3 0 2 1;
#X connect 2 0 5 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X restore 700 400 pd range-check;
#N canvas 40 40 1300 820 realtime-out 0;
#X text 20 20 Every System Real-Time byte leaves here \, on TWO ports. Port 1 is the Launchpad \, which paces its own flash and pulse animations from incoming beat clock \; port 3 is the SP-404 \, whose BPM SYNC time-stretch learns tempo ONLY by measuring pulse intervals. wire.sh already wires Pure Data:4 and Pure Data:6., f 58;
#X obj 20 200 r \$0-rt;
#X obj 20 250 t f f;
#X obj 20 320 midiout;
#X obj 300 320 midiout;
#X obj 700 150 loadbang;
#X obj 700 195 t b b;
#X msg 700 250 1;
#X msg 860 250 3;
#X text 20 420 THE PORT IS SET INTO THE COLD INLET AT LOAD \, which is u_init's proven pattern for the Launchpad SysEx. Pd 0.49 does not warn about extra creation arguments -- [loadbang 7] loads silently -- so a creation argument could not be CONFIRMED to reach anything \, only assumed to. This way there is nothing to assume., f 58;
#X text 20 570 A nonexistent port is silently dropped \, so on the Mac -- where port 3 usually does not exist -- this costs nothing and prints nothing. There is no port-unavailable string in the 0.49 binary at all., f 58;
#X text 20 690 The trigger is not decoration: two destinations is a fan-out \, and undefined order between two MIDI ports is exactly the kind of thing that would only show up as a device drifting., f 58;
#X connect 1 0 2 0;
#X connect 2 0 4 0;
#X connect 2 1 3 0;
#X connect 5 0 6 0;
#X connect 6 0 7 0;
#X connect 6 1 8 0;
#X connect 7 0 3 1;
#X connect 8 0 4 1;
#X restore 300 940 pd realtime-out;
#X obj 900 300 r start;
