#X obj 900 685 s disp;
#X obj 900 760 r panic;
#X obj 900 805 t b b b;
#X msg 1220 870 252;
#X obj 1220 915 s \$0-rt;
#X msg 1040 870 0;
#X obj 1040 915 ctlout 123 33;
#X msg 900 870 led panic;
#X obj 900 915 s disp;
#X obj 1450 300 r \$0-bpm;
#X obj 1450 345 t b f;
#X obj 1450 410 f;
#X obj 1450 455 makefilename %g-bpm;
#X obj 1450 500 list prepend status;
#X obj 1450 545 list trim;
#X obj 1450 590 s disp;
#X obj 1750 180 loadbang;
#X obj 1750 225 t b b;
#X obj 1750 270 del 4000;
#X obj 1900 270 del 200;
#X obj 1900 315 t b b;
#X msg 1900 420 120;
#X obj 1900 465 s tempo;
#X msg 2050 365 led stopped;
#X obj 2050 410 s disp;
#X text 20 1160 WHAT AUDIO-DOMAIN BUYS \, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It does NOT reduce the jitter on the outgoing MIDI byte: threshold~ reports on a 64-sample block boundary exactly as metro fires on one \, so the 1.45 ms is unchanged and is not fixable in vanilla Pd. What it buys is a rate change that is phase-continuous and glitch-free \, and ONE phase that grain-rate code can read as a signal -- so MIDI clock and grain timing cannot drift apart. Written down here because otherwise somebody will try to fix the jitter., f 58;
#X text 20 1360 THE COUNTER RESETS TO 23 \, NOT 0. The next pulse adds one and lands on 0 \, which is the beat. Reset it to 0 and the first beat after every start is silently skipped -- 24 pulses late \, once \, which is exactly the kind of thing nobody notices until a pattern is a beat out., f 58;
#X text 780 1160 CLOCK NEVER STOPS. 248 flows whether the transport is running or not \; only 250 and 252 mark transport. Stop the stream and the 404 stretches its samples to whatever tempo it last measured \, so a stopped clock is a wrong tempo rather than no tempo. This is the least obvious thing in the file., f 44;
#X text 780 1330 NEVER 251. Continue means resume from a stored position and nothing in this rig has Song Position Pointer \, so sending it would be a lie that some future device might believe., f 44;
#X text 780 1460 panic sends 252 and All Notes Off on channel 33 -- port 3 \, channel 1 \, the 404 -- and leaves the clock running. More destinations belong here when anything in Cut It actually emits notes., f 44;
#X text 1450 700 THE FOOTER \, NOT THE PARAM LAYER. status is sticky and always visible \, which is right for a tempo \, and it supersedes u_init's v0.2-ready once the clock is up. panic still overwrites it \, correctly. The f holds the value so the footer can be redrawn without a new tempo arriving., f 44;
#X text 1450 870 TWO LOADBANGS \, TWO REASONS. del 200 publishes 120 on tempo ONCE -- the only time this file writes that bus -- so the phasor starts and anything that came up first still gets a value. del 4000 redraws the footer only \, because u_init's boot sequence puts v0.2-ready there at about 3.5 s. THAT NUMBER IS COUPLED TO u_init'S TIMINGS and u_init says so too., f 44;
#X text 1450 1070 It stores what it hears and never re-emits \, so there is no loop between this file and the bus. Anything may write tempo \; only this file owns what happens next., f 44;
#X text 300 1000 THREE RECEIVES \, ONE NAME \, AND THE ORDER DOES NOT MATTER -- which is why they are receives and not a trigger. All three write COLD inlets and none of them fires anything: the phase \, the threshold~ state and the counter are simply put back where a start wants them \, and the next block does the rest., f 44;
#X text 620 1000 threshold~ takes a 0 in its right inlet to force the state low \, with no debounce -- straight out of its help patch. Without it a start arriving while the phasor is above 0.5 would wait for a wrap before the first pulse., f 30;
#X text 1450 1250 BOTH DEBOUNCES ARE ZERO \, AND THAT IS A MEASURED DECISION RATHER THAN A TIDY DEFAULT. threshold~ decrements its dead time once per DSP BLOCK \, not per millisecond \, so ANY non-zero debounce burns a whole 1.45 ms block on every state change: at 2 ms the clock silently lost pulses above about 430 BPM \, reporting 17 beats where 25 were due. At zero the limit is two blocks per pulse -- 344 Hz \, measured \, which is 44100/64/2 to the digit. That is 860 BPM \, so the clamp of 600 above has 43 percent of headroom. A phasor cannot bounce \, so there was never anything for a debounce to protect against., f 44;
#X obj 1330 410 s \$0-refresh;
#X obj 1330 640 s \$0-refresh;
#X obj 1620 420 r \$0-refresh;
#X text 1900 1250 THE FOOTER IS REDRAWN ON EVERY TRANSPORT CHANGE \, and that is not cosmetic. status is STICKY \, so panic sat in the footer until the next tempo message -- you could start the transport \, watch the LED go green and the 404 start \, and still be told panic. Found on the device. A start or a stop now bangs the stored BPM back out \, so the footer always describes the state you are actually in., f 44;
#X obj 1900 370 spigot 1;
#X obj 2280 225 r tempo;
#X obj 2280 270 t b;
#X msg 2280 315 0;
#X text 2280 380 THE 120 IS A FALLBACK \, NOT A DEFAULT \, and this spigot is what makes the difference. mother pushes the real knob positions at load \, so knob 1 usually sets the tempo before this fires -- but WHICH ARRIVES FIRST IS A RACE \, and it was seen going both ways on the same device: booting at 10 BPM one day and 120 the next. Any tempo at all closes this gate \, so the seed can only ever fill a silence. It still matters on the Mac \, where nothing pushes a knob position at all., f 50;
